WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
Film Courage: Bill, if you could pick five
books that you would be allowed to read every

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:11.700
year and only those five books, what would
they be?

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:19.410
Bill Duke, actor, director, author: Oh, Gosh.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:26.010
I love books, Im just trying…five?

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.110
Man…

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:34.060
Film Courage: Sorry, I know that’s limiting.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:51.410
Bill Duke: An Empire Of Their Own [Author
Neal Gabler]

00:00:51.410 --> 00:01:04.230
What is the 
book I always talk about?

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:10.210
[Bill turns to his assistant off-camera]?

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.190
1984 [Author George Orwell].

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:28.980
But the one about the mythology and all the
major studio guys read this book, The Hero

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.350
With A Thousand Faces [Author Joseph Campbell].

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:42.810
That is an incredible book about all the stories
of the world have a certain commonality, what

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.390
they are.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:50.500
Steven Spielberg used it, [George] Lucas uses
it, everybody uses it…The Hero’s Journey,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.040
what is the hero’s journey?

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:57.780
And there is a commonality of every hero’s
journey.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:10.780
I read a book by Steven Pressfield that I
read as often as I can and it’s called The

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.780
War of Art.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.940
It’s a take off of Sun Tzu the Art of War.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:28.590
And it talks about 
what is it that stops the average person from

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.890
accomplishing anything they want to accomplish.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.760
The book is this thick [indicates a thin book]
and the book pisses you off so bad because

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:49.040
from the first page you say to yourself “He’s
talking about me.”

00:02:49.040 --> 00:03:03.580
And he says the father and mother of incompetence
is procrastination and the rationalizations

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:12.630
we come up with to justify a procrastination.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:22.310
Hours and days of thinking of thinking why
we are not doing it and justifying it instead

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.910
of doing it.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:31.880
He says it’s easier to know the hand that
is choking you then to let another hand in.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.680
This has been choking me for years.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.650
I’m not dead but you’re asking me to put
another hand near me?

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:48.100
I don’t know this, I know this [imitates
himself holding himself back around his neck]

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.450
because that’s money.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:52.260
It’s a brilliant, brilliant, brilliant book.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:04:09.260
And then there are books, I forgot the author’s
name right now…one is called The Dip, one

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.160
is called The Purple Cow.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.110
Seth Godin?

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:16.600
Oh!

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.600
Is he great or not?

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.600
Film Courage: He has a podcast, too?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.379
Bill Duke: He’s brilliant.

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:23.129
The books are this thick.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:26.800
But he tells you and shows you accomplishment.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.790
What is accomplishment?

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:36.370
That’s four and then my favorite poet is
T.S.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.870
Eliot’s Four Quartets.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:50.430
It’s the last thing I think before he passed
and he talks about how every piece of poetry

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:56.729
but before that is meaningless until he just
says right now he talks about what he has

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:06.490
learned in life and puts it in poem format.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:13.860
Federico Fellini and Frank Capra are two of
my favorite directors of all time.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:22.999
8 AND A 1/2 every year I watch and sometimes
look at the script of MR.

00:05:22.999 --> 00:05:28.779
SMITH GOES TO WASHINGTON and IT’S A WONDERFUL
LIFE.

00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:36.129
The messages, the courage that he had at that
time and when he was tortured by McCarthy

00:05:36.129 --> 00:05:40.589
for saying the things he thinks he said and
accusing him of being a communist but he went

00:05:40.589 --> 00:05:52.879
on and did it anyway, he has encouraged my
career and I can’t remember the name of

00:05:52.879 --> 00:06:00.180
the director now but the script I could read
over and over and over again because it’s

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.249
so brilliant and the film I could watch over
a thousand times because it was so brilliant

00:06:04.249 --> 00:06:10.069
came out about 9 or 10 years ago called RUN
LOLA RUN [German thriller film written and

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:11.069
directed by Tom Tykwer].

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:12.069
Have you ever seen that one?

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:13.909
Film Courage: I’ve seen the trailer.

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:16.249
Bill Duke: You’ve got to watch it!

00:06:16.249 --> 00:06:18.249
Film Courage: It’s on my list.

00:06:18.249 --> 00:06:19.909
Bill Duke: He’s a genius.

00:06:19.909 --> 00:06:30.809
When I say genius, he never got the credit
he deserved but the film is told pictorially

00:06:30.809 --> 00:06:38.280
and structurally through methodologies that
no one else has ever seen that way.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:49.900
It’s like…I’ll give you one example
it’s a 24 hour thing.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:55.081
This woman is called by her boyfriend supposed
to deliver to the dealer doesn't have their

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:59.080
it set the money to the dealer because he
lost the package on

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:03.979
the plane and named dealers coming in like
three two or three hours and

00:07:03.979 --> 00:07:08.319
he's got to be at a store and you have a pharmacy
to meet the dealer

00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:11.919
if the drug sees now the money he's gonna
get hurt.

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:18.009
So his girlfriend
does everything she can to make sure so the

00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:23.490
film is divided in to all the
attempts she makes and it starts her back

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:29.819
at the apartment over and over
again and runs down the steps to different

00:07:29.819 --> 00:07:37.210
places now as she runs down
the steps she must down the steps she's running

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:42.300
doors open to a older lady's apartment and
it moves over the older lady

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:50.619
shoulder into the TV and the TV shows are
here opening down the steps when she gets

00:07:50.619 --> 00:07:59.589
in the street she passes people and everybody
she passes you see in five seconds a flashback

00:07:59.589 --> 00:08:06.119
of their entire lives who they are
and when she gets to the place where she tries

00:08:06.119 --> 00:08:12.649
to get the money for him and it
fails you go back to the appointment the phone

00:08:12.649 --> 00:08:18.490
rings and he says I'm gonna get hurt if you
don't get here she gets up and she once again

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:21.150
to try to get the money from someplace else.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:26.479
It’s called RUN LOLA RUN.

